window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][53] = "<h1>Part IX — Behavioral and Communication</h1>\n<span id=\"c31\"></span>\n<h2>C31 — Behavioral Interviews: Stories That Hold Up</h2>\n<span id=\"c31-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;Tell me about yourself.&quot; &quot;Why product management?&quot; &quot;Tell me about a time you disagreed with your manager.&quot; &quot;Tell me about a decision you got wrong.&quot; &quot;Describe a time you protected users against a business request.&quot; The behavioral round is often the gate that decides an offer after strong product and analytical rounds, because it tests what the others cannot: how you actually behave with people, under pressure, with incomplete information and real consequences. Interviewers cannot watch you work, so they ask for evidence from your past and judge it for what it reveals. This chapter is a method for building stories from your own work that are specific, honest, well chosen and able to survive probing.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: behavioral and &quot;leadership&quot; rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others, often one or more dedicated rounds plus behavioral openers in every other round, and a hiring-committee or hiring-manager conversation <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Several companies publish the attributes or values they assess (C31.1). For a candidate with about two years of experience, the round tests judgment and growth more than scale: what you owned, how you thought, what you did when it was hard, and what you learned.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> The <strong>signal</strong> your answer gives. A <strong>signal</strong> is what an interviewer infers about your judgment, skills, values or growth from an answer. A story with no specifics gives no signal; a story where you made a real decision, took a real action and learned something real gives a lot. Interviewers listen for ownership (you, not only the team), judgment (why this option), results with evidence, honesty about mistakes, concern for users and colleagues, and the ability to reflect and change.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You introduce yourself in about two minutes with a clear arc and three proof points.</li>\n<li>You give honest, specific reasons for product management and for the company and role.</li>\n<li>You tell stories with a clear context, decision, action, result and learning, in two to three minutes.</li>\n<li>You separate what you did from what the team did, and you give credit.</li>\n<li>You answer probes three levels deep with concrete detail, and you do not inflate.</li>\n<li>You handle failure, weakness, hypotheticals and values scenarios with judgment and without evasion.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end behavioral answers: a rambling background with no arc, a generic motive (&quot;I love solving problems&quot;), a story told entirely as &quot;we,&quot; a result with no number, a &quot;weakness&quot; that is a disguised strength, and a values question answered with what you think the interviewer wants to hear.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-1-what-the-round-tests-attribute-maps\"></span>\n<h4>C31.1 What the round tests; attribute maps</h4>\n<p>Interviewers use a short list of attributes, sometimes published, to organize their notes (FW-C31-01). Where a company publishes its list, preparing against it is sensible; where it does not, any claim about what it assesses is inference.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Company</th>\n<th>What is published or reported</th>\n<th>Label</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Amazon</td>\n<td>Sixteen Leadership Principles on its careers site, including Customer Obsession, Ownership, Invent and Simplify, Are Right A Lot, Bias for Action, Frugality, Earn Trust, Dive Deep, Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit, and Deliver Results; the list grew from fourteen to sixteen in 2021</td>\n<td><code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google</td>\n<td>Careers pages describe attributes assessed in hiring, commonly given as general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and an attribute called &quot;Googleyness&quot;, which Google has said should not be confused with culture fit</td>\n<td><code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Work Rules; The Information on the Googleyness clarification]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meta</td>\n<td>Published company values emphasize moving fast, focusing on long-term impact and direct communication</td>\n<td><code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta's careers culture page]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Microsoft</td>\n<td>Culture materials emphasize a growth mindset, customer focus and collaboration</td>\n<td><code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Netflix</td>\n<td>A published culture memo (June 2024) with four principles, candor as a value, context not control and the keeper test</td>\n<td><code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stripe</td>\n<td>Published operating principles (for example, users first, and move with urgency and focus)</td>\n<td><code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe careers pages, principles list; also quoted by The Pragmatic Engineer]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apple</td>\n<td>No published rubric; candidates commonly report an emphasis on craft, collaboration, discretion and attention to detail</td>\n<td><code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>How to use a map.</strong> <em>Stories first, labels second.</em> Build stories from your real work (C31.5), then see which attributes each shows. Do not invent stories to fit a list, and do not recite principle names in your answers. A story about disagreeing with a manager and then committing shows an Amazon-style attribute without your saying so; naming the principle adds nothing and can sound rehearsed. Use the map to check coverage and to choose which stories to lead with in a given loop.</p>\n<p><strong>What nearly every company tests, whatever it is called.</strong> Ownership (you took responsibility), judgment (you chose among real options with reasons), working with others (conflict, influence, feedback), customer or user focus, learning from failure, and integrity under pressure.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-2-your-two-minute-introduction\"></span>\n<h4>C31.2 Your two-minute introduction</h4>\n<p>&quot;Tell me about yourself&quot; and &quot;walk me through your background&quot; are the most predictable questions and the most often wasted. A good answer is about two minutes (roughly 260 to 300 words at a natural pace), has an arc, and plants three proof points that the interviewer can probe later (FW-C31-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Structure.</strong> <em>Present:</em> what you do now, in one or two sentences with scope (&quot;I'm a product analyst on a team of six responsible for onboarding for a budgeting app used by about 400,000 people&quot;). <em>Past:</em> two or three steps that explain how you got here, each with a reason and a proof point (a result, a decision you owned). <em>Future:</em> why this role and why now, tied to what you have shown. End by handing over: &quot;I'm happy to go deeper on any of those.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Choose proof points</strong> that are specific, recent and relevant to the role, and that you can talk about for five minutes. Each should hint at a story in your bank. Leave out: your whole résumé in order, school detail beyond a clause, apologies (&quot;I only have two years&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Variants.</strong> &quot;Walk me through your résumé&quot; invites the same arc with the order of your résumé; &quot;tell me about a project you're proud of&quot; is a story question (C31.4). Worked example 1 rewrites a weak introduction into a strong one.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-3-why-product-management-and-why-this-company-and-role\"></span>\n<h4>C31.3 Why product management, and why this company and role</h4>\n<p><strong>Why product management.</strong> Interviewers have heard &quot;I like solving problems&quot; and &quot;I'm a bridge between teams&quot; thousands of times, and they give no signal. A good answer points to evidence: moments in your work when you were drawn to the problem of what to build and why; what you did about it; and what you learned about the work (the unglamorous parts included: prioritizing, saying no, living with ambiguity). Test it: could someone who does not want to be a PM say the same words? If so, add specifics.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this company and role.</strong> Name one or two concrete reasons that connect something real about the company (a product you use and have views on, a published principle you act on, a problem space) to something real about you (a past decision, a skill). Avoid flattery (&quot;you're the best&quot;) and facts everyone knows. Show you did the work: you have read the product's recent changes, tried it, and can say what you would examine first in the role. Honest reasons about career growth are fine if they are paired with what you offer.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;What is your favorite product of ours?&quot;</strong> This tests taste and candor. Choose a real favorite you actually use, say who it is for and what job it does, name two or three specific design decisions you admire and why they serve users, and then give one honest critique with how you would improve it (C11's teardown method: observation, inference, opinion). Do not choose a product to flatter; do not criticize it as if you knew internal constraints. For an Apple-type question, &quot;what is a product detail you admire and what did it cost to get right?&quot; is the craft-and-tradeoff version (IQ-C31-05).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to over-prepare the script.</strong> A memorized motive reads as one. Prepare the evidence and the structure; let the words vary.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-4-story-anatomy-context-decision-action-result-learning\"></span>\n<h4>C31.4 Story anatomy: context, decision, action, result, learning</h4>\n<p>A well-formed story answers the question in about two to three minutes (FW-C31-03). A common structure, widely recommended in interview preparation, is STAR (situation, task, action, result) <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Widely recommended in interview preparation (Glassdoor guide; consulting and Amazon-focused prep sites); some sites claim it originated at Amazon, which these sources do not establish and the book does not claim]</code>. It is a good skeleton and has a weakness: it can bury the decision and the learning. A stronger version for PM roles:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Context (about 20% of the time).</strong> The situation, the stakes and the constraint, in two or three sentences: who was affected, what was at risk, what made it hard. Include one number if you can.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision (20%).</strong> The choice that was yours: the options you saw and why you chose one. This is the center of a PM story, and the part most candidates skip.</li>\n<li><strong>Action (30%).</strong> What <em>you</em> did, step by step, in the first person: whom you talked to, what you built or analyzed, how you persuaded or unblocked. Credit the others where due, and be clear on your part.</li>\n<li><strong>Result (20%).</strong> What happened, with evidence: a number with its baseline, or an honest &quot;I don't have the final number; here is what I do know.&quot; Include what did not work.</li>\n<li><strong>Learning (10%).</strong> What you would do differently, and what has changed in how you work since. Concrete and behavioral, not a platitude.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On a 150-second answer those shares are about 30, 30, 45, 30 and 15 seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;I&quot; and &quot;we.&quot;</strong> Interviewers hire you, not your team. Use &quot;we&quot; for team outcomes and shared decisions, and &quot;I&quot; for your decisions and actions; if you use only &quot;we,&quot; they cannot tell what you did, and if you use only &quot;I,&quot; you sound like you did not work with anyone. A useful test: for each &quot;we,&quot; could you say what you personally did?</p>\n<p><strong>Specifics.</strong> Numbers, names (of roles, not private people), dates, tools and quotes make a story credible: &quot;the signup form had 12 fields and 41% of people stopped before the end,&quot; not &quot;the signup flow was bad.&quot; Every figure must be true; invented detail is the quickest way to fail a probe (C31.7).</p>\n<p><strong>Honesty about confidentiality.</strong> Describe the situation without revealing confidential details (use ranges, relative changes, generic names for customers). If you cannot share a number, say so and give a relative one.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-5-building-a-story-bank-from-real-work\"></span>\n<h4>C31.5 Building a story bank from real work</h4>\n<p>A <strong>story bank</strong> is a prepared set of real experiences, each with notes, mapped to the themes interviewers ask about, from which you choose stories for each question. The point is coverage with a manageable number of stories, each rehearsed and fact-checked (FW-C31-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Steps.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Inventory.</em> List 12 to 15 episodes from the last two years (more if you have them): projects, launches, disagreements, mistakes, moments of ambiguity, times you helped someone, times you changed your mind. Include work beyond your job title: internships, campus and community projects, hackathons, volunteering, side projects. For someone with about two years of experience, the best stories often come from the first project you owned, a launch that went wrong, or a disagreement you handled.</li>\n<li><em>Select.</em> Keep episodes where (a) you made a real decision; (b) the stakes were clear; (c) you can state an outcome with evidence; (d) you learned something; (e) you can tell it truthfully in two to three minutes and for ten more under probing.</li>\n<li><em>Map to themes.</em> Conflict, failure, influence without authority, ambiguity, customer focus, a data-driven decision, prioritization, leading others, and a values moment (C31.6). Most strong stories fit two or three themes with different emphasis. Aim to cover each theme with at least one story and the most common (conflict, failure, influence) with two, so that you never repeat a story within a loop.</li>\n<li><em>Write notes, not scripts.</em> For each story: one line each for context, decision, action, result and learning; the numbers; the names of roles; two likely probes and their answers; and what to leave out. Scripts sound memorized.</li>\n<li><em>Check the facts.</em> Verify numbers and dates against your records; remember who did what; confirm what you can say publicly.</li>\n<li><em>Rehearse aloud</em> and time each at two to three minutes (C31.11).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Worked example 4 builds a bank for a candidate with two years of experience and shows the coverage matrix.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-6-story-types\"></span>\n<h4>C31.6 Story types</h4>\n<p>Eight themes cover most behavioral questions. For each, what the interviewer is looking for and the common trap:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Theme</th>\n<th>Typical prompt</th>\n<th>Looking for</th>\n<th>Trap</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conflict</td>\n<td>&quot;A time you disagreed with a colleague&quot;</td>\n<td>Respect, listening, a real resolution, what you learned about the other person's view</td>\n<td>A story where you were simply right; a villain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td>&quot;A decision you got wrong&quot;</td>\n<td>Ownership, real stakes, a clear lesson with changed behavior</td>\n<td>A failure that is a disguised success; blame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Influence without authority</td>\n<td>&quot;A time you got people to act without being their manager&quot;</td>\n<td>Understanding incentives, evidence, building allies</td>\n<td>&quot;I just explained it and they agreed&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ambiguity</td>\n<td>&quot;A time you had unclear goals or little information&quot;</td>\n<td>Structuring, assumptions, cheap tests, iteration</td>\n<td>Waiting for clarity; no method</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer or user focus</td>\n<td>&quot;A time you went deep on a user problem&quot;</td>\n<td>Direct contact with users, insight, a change driven by it</td>\n<td>Customer as an abstraction; no user contact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data-driven decision</td>\n<td>&quot;A decision you made with data&quot;</td>\n<td>Right question, sound analysis, limits of the data, a decision</td>\n<td>A dashboard read-out; no decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prioritization</td>\n<td>&quot;A time you had to say no or cut scope&quot;</td>\n<td>Criteria, tradeoffs, communication, impact</td>\n<td>Everything was important; no cost named</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leading others</td>\n<td>&quot;A time you led a team or a project&quot;</td>\n<td>Clarity, delegation, development of others, accountability</td>\n<td>Doing everything yourself; credit-taking</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A ninth category, <em>values and judgment</em>, is treated in C31.10. Many questions combine themes (&quot;a time you influenced a decision using data when you disagreed&quot;), so choose a story that can carry the combination.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-7-handling-follow-up-probes-three-levels-deep\"></span>\n<h4>C31.7 Handling follow-up probes three levels deep</h4>\n<p>Interviewers probe to test whether the story is real and whether you understand it (FW-C31-05). Expect questions at three levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Level 1, the detail:</em> &quot;What exactly did you do?&quot; &quot;Who was involved?&quot; &quot;How long did it take?&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Level 2, the reasoning:</em> &quot;Why that option and not another?&quot; &quot;What data did you use?&quot; &quot;What did the other person say?&quot; &quot;How did you know it worked?&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Level 3, the counterfactual and the limits:</em> &quot;What would you do differently?&quot; &quot;What if the numbers had been the other way?&quot; &quot;What did you not know at the time?&quot; &quot;Was the result due to your decision or something else?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to prepare.</strong> For each story, write the answers to two probes at each level. Know your numbers, the options you rejected, and one thing that went wrong.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked for a time they influenced a decision, a candidate says they convinced a director with data and that conversion rose 15%. The interviewer asks what the baseline was and what the director first said. The candidate cannot recall either and changes the number twice. The story may have been true; the probes showed that the candidate did not know it well enough to tell it. A better answer: prepare the baseline and the objection beforehand, and when unsure say &quot;I don't recall exactly; it was roughly a quarter&quot; and move on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>How to answer.</strong> Pause, then answer the question asked, concretely, in the first person. If you do not remember, say so (&quot;I don't recall the exact figure; it was roughly a quarter of users&quot;) and give what you do know. Never invent to fill a gap: a made-up number is discovered by the next probe. If a probe exposes a flaw in your decision, acknowledge it and say what you now think; that is signal of judgment and growth, not failure. Do not become defensive. The probes are the real interview: the first two minutes get you the question, the next ten decide the evaluation <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-8-failure-and-weakness-answers\"></span>\n<h4>C31.8 Failure and weakness answers</h4>\n<p><strong>Failure.</strong> The best failure stories have a real cost (money, time, trust, a missed launch), a decision that was yours, an honest account of why you made it, and a learning that changed your behavior, with evidence that it did (FW-C31-06). Avoid failures that are not failures (&quot;I worked too hard&quot;), failures that were someone else's fault, and failures so catastrophic that they raise doubts about your judgment. A good choice for an early-career candidate is a mistake in an analysis, a launch you rushed, or a stakeholder you failed to bring along, told with its cost and the habit you changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure.</strong> Context and stakes; what you decided and why it seemed right then; what went wrong and how you found out; what you did to limit the damage and repair it; what you learned; and the specific thing you do differently now, with an example of the new habit in action.</p>\n<p><strong>Weakness.</strong> &quot;What is your biggest weakness?&quot; asks for self-awareness. Choose a real weakness that does not disqualify you for this role, say how it shows up with an example, what you are doing about it, and what progress you can show. &quot;I take on too much&quot; and &quot;I'm a perfectionist&quot; are rehearsed and signal evasion. A real, specific one (&quot;I tend to start with analysis when a quick user conversation would settle it faster; I now set a rule to talk to three users before building a model&quot;) signals judgment.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-9-situational-hypothetical-questions\"></span>\n<h4>C31.9 Situational (hypothetical) questions</h4>\n<p>&quot;What would you do if...&quot; questions ask for your reasoning in a scenario (FW-C31-07). Unlike behavioral questions, there is no past to draw on, so the interviewer assesses your process and values. A structure: <em>clarify</em> the facts you would want (two or three questions); <em>state the principle or priority</em> that guides you (users, honesty, the goal); <em>lay out the options</em> and their tradeoffs; <em>say what you would do first</em>, with reasons; and <em>say how you would check</em> whether it worked and what would change your mind. If you have faced something similar, say so and give a short example (&quot;I faced something like this once; here is what I did and what I would do now&quot;): real experience is stronger than theory.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfalls.</strong> Answering with a single rule (&quot;I'd escalate&quot;), refusing the premise, or giving a long list of possible actions without choosing. Choose and justify.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-10-values-and-judgment-scenarios-ethics-safety-privacy-p\"></span>\n<h4>C31.10 Values and judgment scenarios: ethics, safety, privacy, pressure to hit a number</h4>\n<p>These questions test integrity under pressure: &quot;Your manager wants a growth tactic you think is manipulative,&quot; &quot;Describe a time you protected users' privacy against a business request,&quot; &quot;You learn of a defect that could hurt users the day before launch.&quot; They are about what you will do when it costs you something. FW-C31-08 gives a frame.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Facts.</strong> What do you actually know, and what are you assuming? Check before acting.</li>\n<li><strong>Stakeholders and harms.</strong> Who could be hurt (users, bystanders, the company), how badly, and how reversibly?</li>\n<li><strong>The pressure.</strong> What is driving the request (a target, a deadline), and is the goal legitimate by another route?</li>\n<li><strong>Options.</strong> Not only &quot;comply&quot; or &quot;refuse&quot;: a safer version, a delay with a plan, a test that measures the real effect, an escalation.</li>\n<li><strong>Principle and policy.</strong> What does the law, the company policy or the product's stated principles say? (C10.10 on dark patterns, C25 on governance.)</li>\n<li><strong>Action and escalation.</strong> What you would do, whom you would involve, in what order, and how you would communicate it honestly.</li>\n<li><strong>The cost you accept.</strong> Say what it might cost you (a missed target, a hard conversation) and why you accept it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What interviewers want.</strong> Not a speech about integrity, but a decision with a cost, a route to the legitimate goal, and respect for the people involved. CS-C31-02 shows a real company decision with a large, known cost.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-11-delivery-length-tone-signposting\"></span>\n<h4>C31.11 Delivery: length, tone, signposting</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Length.</strong> Two to three minutes per story; ninety seconds to two minutes for the introduction. Longer answers lose the interviewer; much shorter ones lack detail. Stop when you have made your point and invite probing.</li>\n<li><strong>Signposting.</strong> Tell the listener where you are: &quot;The situation was..., the decision I had to make was..., what I did was..., the result was..., and what I learned was...&quot; Signposts help the interviewer take notes, which is what they will be judged on.</li>\n<li><strong>Tone.</strong> Calm, direct, conversational; confident about what you did and open about what you did not know. Avoid jargon the interviewer may not share, avoid self-congratulation and avoid self-deprecation.</li>\n<li><strong>Pace and pause.</strong> A short pause before answering is fine and shows thought. Speak at a natural pace; nerves speed people up.</li>\n<li><strong>Video and remote.</strong> Look at the camera for key points, keep a glass of water, and keep a short list of your story headlines out of view of the screen share.</li>\n<li><strong>Not memorized.</strong> Rehearse until you know the structure and the facts, not the sentences.</li>\n<li><strong>Listen for the question.</strong> Answer what was asked; if you offer a story for a different theme, say so briefly.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c31-c31-12-worked-stories-at-three-bands\"></span>\n<h4>C31.12 Worked stories at three bands</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked examples, built as stories with notes and probes (all <code>Illustrative</code> composites with invented details), and section 6 shows one prompt answered at three bands (Baseline, Strong, Standout). Section 11 gives twelve questions with model answers, probes and answers to them.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The problem-definition template (C02.7), the decision memo (FW-C02-06), the pre-mortem (FW-C02-05), the teardown method (C11) and the scope-negotiation script (FW-C15-07) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-01 · The attribute map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a target company and what it publishes or what candidates report.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a short list of attributes labeled <code>Public</code> or <code>Inferred</code>, each mapped to one or more stories from your bank, with coverage gaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the list is checked against the current source <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a map guides coverage and tempts you to recite labels.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> inventing stories to fit; reciting principle names; treating inference as published fact.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and C31.1.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attribute</th>\n<th>Published or inferred</th>\n<th>Story that shows it</th>\n<th>Gap</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-02 · The two-minute introduction · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> your last two to three roles and one target role.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a 260 to 300 word introduction with present (scope), past (two or three steps with reasons), three proof points that hint at stories, and a future tied to the role.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can speak about each proof point for five minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a tight arc omits much of your history.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> reciting the résumé; no proof points; generic motive.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Present with scope → Past in two or three steps, each with a reason and a proof point → Future tied to this role → Hand over.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-03 · Story anatomy: context, decision, action, result, learning · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an episode from your work.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a story in five parts with time shares (20, 20, 30, 20 and 10 percent of two to three minutes), numbers with baselines, a clear &quot;I&quot; and &quot;we&quot;, and a behavioral learning.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you made a real decision and can state an outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure shows judgment and can sound mechanical if scripted.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> no decision; all &quot;we&quot;; no numbers; a generic lesson.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What it contains</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Context</td>\n<td>Stakes, constraint, one number</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision</td>\n<td>Options and why this one</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action</td>\n<td>What you did, in order, with credit</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Result</td>\n<td>Evidence, baseline, what did not work</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning</td>\n<td>What changed in how you work</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-04 · The story-bank matrix · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> 12 to 15 episodes from your work and the eight themes (plus values).</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a matrix of episodes by theme with the strongest theme and one backup per episode, selection criteria, notes per story (five lines, numbers, two probes with answers, what to omit), and a coverage check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> facts are verified and shareable.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a larger bank covers more and costs more rehearsal time; eight to ten rehearsed stories is usually enough.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a bank of scripts; one story for every theme; unverified numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-05 · The probe ladder · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a story.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> two probes at each of three levels (detail, reasoning, counterfactual and limits) with prepared answers, the numbers at hand, and one thing that went wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you know the story well enough to be questioned for ten minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> preparing probes takes time and prevents panic.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> inventing facts under pressure; defensiveness; changing the story's facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3 and C31.7.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Probe</th>\n<th>Answer notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Detail</td>\n<td>What exactly did you do?</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 Reasoning</td>\n<td>Why that option? What data?</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 Limits</td>\n<td>What would you do differently? What did you not know?</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-06 · The failure-story test · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a candidate failure story.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a pass or fail on five tests: real cost, your decision, an honest reason it seemed right, repair, and a behavior change with an example of it in use.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can tell it without blame.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a real failure is uncomfortable and more credible.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a disguised success; blaming others; no changed behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3 and IQ-C31-08.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-07 · The situational answer · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a &quot;what would you do if&quot; prompt.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with clarifying questions, the guiding principle, options and tradeoffs, a chosen first action with reasons, and a check on whether it worked.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer a clarifying question or two.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a structured answer may be longer than the interviewer wants; signpost and stop.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a single rule; refusing the premise; a list with no choice.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C31-11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify → Principle → Options and tradeoffs → First action → How I'd check.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c31-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C31-08 · The values scenario frame · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a scenario with a conflict between a goal and an ethical or safety concern.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the facts and assumptions, stakeholders and harms (and reversibility), the pressure and the legitimate goal behind it, options beyond comply or refuse, relevant policy or principles, an action with escalation and honest communication, and the cost you accept.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you would act on what you say.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> integrity can cost short-term results; naming the cost makes the answer credible.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a speech; a binary; a hero story with no cost; a rule recited without judgment.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5, IQ-C31-06, IQ-C31-11 and CS-C31-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Facts</td>\n<td>What do I know and assume?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stakeholders and harms</td>\n<td>Who could be hurt, how badly, how reversibly?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pressure and goal</td>\n<td>What is driving it; can the goal be met another way?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options</td>\n<td>What else besides comply or refuse?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Principle</td>\n<td>What do law, policy and product principles say?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action</td>\n<td>What first, whom to involve, how to communicate?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td>What might this cost me, and why accept it?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c31-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c31-the-behavioral-answer-for-a-45-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The behavioral answer, for a 45-minute round</h4>\n<p>A behavioral round is usually three to five questions with probes. A typical flow: a two-minute introduction (or a short one), then three to four story questions of about three minutes each, each followed by five to eight minutes of probing, and your questions at the end.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Opening: introduction or &quot;why us&quot;</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The two-minute arc with three proof points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Story 1 with probes</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Context, decision, action, result, learning; then three levels of probes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Story 2 with probes</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A different theme; same structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Story 3 with probes</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A failure or values story; honest, specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Situational or values question</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The frame; a decision with a cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Your questions</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Two or three real questions about the role and team</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrap and buffer</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Close; room for an interviewer's follow-up</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> Keep each initial answer to about three minutes and let the interviewer probe. If you run long, cut the context and keep the decision, your action and the learning. If the interviewer interrupts with a probe, answer it, then return briefly to the thread.</p>\n<p><strong>How to start an answer.</strong> Name the story in a sentence and say which question it answers: &quot;I'll tell you about a launch I led that went wrong, since it taught me the most about speed and quality.&quot; Then context.</p>\n<p><strong>How to close an answer.</strong> One sentence of learning, then stop: &quot;What changed for me is that I now agree the quality bar in writing before the date is set.&quot; Invite probing: &quot;Happy to go into any part.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-type-introduction-motive-and-favorite-product\"></span>\n<h4>Type: introduction, motive and favorite product</h4>\n<p>Use the two-minute arc, evidence for the motive, and the favorite-product formula (user, job, two decisions, one critique).</p>\n<span id=\"c31-type-a-story-prompt-conflict-influence-ambiguity-customer-da\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a story prompt (conflict, influence, ambiguity, customer, data, prioritization, leading)</h4>\n<p>Choose a bank story; context, decision, action, result, learning; prepare for probes.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-type-failure-or-weakness\"></span>\n<h4>Type: failure or weakness</h4>\n<p>Apply the failure-story test; real cost, owned decision, changed behavior.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-type-situational-what-would-you-do-if\"></span>\n<h4>Type: situational (what would you do if)</h4>\n<p>Clarify, principle, options, first action, how you'd check.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-type-values-and-judgment\"></span>\n<h4>Type: values and judgment</h4>\n<p>The values frame: facts, harms, goal behind the pressure, options, principle, action, cost accepted.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What was your role?&quot;</td>\n<td>Too much &quot;we&quot;</td>\n<td>State your decisions and actions in &quot;I&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why did you choose that?&quot;</td>\n<td>Reasoning probe</td>\n<td>Name the options and the reason; admit the tradeoff</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What was the result?&quot;</td>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Give the number and baseline, or say what you know</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What would you do differently?&quot;</td>\n<td>Learning</td>\n<td>A concrete behavior, with an example of using it since</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Was that because of you?&quot;</td>\n<td>Attribution</td>\n<td>Separate your contribution from other causes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Have you faced something like this?&quot;</td>\n<td>Situational asks for a past</td>\n<td>Offer a short real example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if the number were worse?&quot;</td>\n<td>Limits</td>\n<td>Say what you would conclude and do</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c31-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code> composites: the people, companies, numbers and events are invented (invented), built to show structure, and they must never be used as your own experience. Your stories must be true. Each shows the story as notes and as it might be told, with the signals an interviewer would take from it.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-1-from-a-rambling-introduction-to-a-two-minut\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — From a rambling introduction to a two-minute arc (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The weak version (summarized).</strong> &quot;So I did my degree in economics, and then I did an internship, and then I got a job at a payments company where I worked on lots of things, dashboards mostly, and also some SQL, and then I moved to my current company, and now I work on a team that does onboarding, and I'm looking to move to product management because I like working with people and solving problems.&quot; It runs four minutes when spoken in full, mentions no result, and ends with a generic motive (invented). The interviewer learns where the candidate has been and nothing about what they can do.</p>\n<p><strong>Rebuild it (FW-C31-02).</strong> <em>Present with scope.</em> What you own and how big it is. <em>Past in two or three steps,</em> each with a reason and a proof point. <em>Future tied to the role.</em> <em>Hand over.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The strong version (237 words, about 1 minute 50 seconds at an unhurried pace).</strong> &quot;I'm a product analyst on the onboarding team at a budgeting app with about 400,000 monthly users, and I own the metrics and experiments for the first seven days. I got here in three steps. I studied economics, and in my final year I ran a survey project for a campus startup, where I found I liked deciding what to ask more than analyzing the answers. That led to my first job, as an analyst at a payments company. I built the first funnel dashboard for merchant sign-up and found that 38% of merchants stopped at bank verification. I took it to the product manager, and with a designer we cut the form from 12 fields to 7; completion rose from 52% to 61%.</p>\n<p>That was when I realized I wanted to own the decision, not only the analysis. Two years ago I moved to my current team, where I run about two experiments a month. The one I am proudest of changed our default notification settings: I showed that the old default raised opens and also doubled opt-outs, and the quieter default we shipped raised 90-day retention by 1.2 points. What I want next is to own a product area end to end, and that is why this role interests me: the team works on helping first-time investors, a problem I have spent the last year studying. I'm happy to go deeper on any of this.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What the arc does.</strong> Scope in the first sentence; three steps, each with a reason (&quot;I liked deciding what to ask&quot;); two proof points with numbers (the form change, the notification default) that invite probes; a motive supported by evidence; a hand-over. The interviewer now has two stories to ask about, which the candidate has prepared.</p>\n<p><strong>What to check.</strong> Every number must be true and shareable. Each proof point must be a story the candidate can tell for five more minutes with a decision, an action and a learning. If the form-change story was mostly the product manager's, the candidate says what they personally did.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> An introduction is a trailer for the stories: scope, an arc with reasons, and proof points that give the interviewer something to ask.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-2-a-conflict-story-built-from-notes-illustrat\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — A conflict story built from notes (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Tell me about a time you disagreed with a colleague.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>The notes (FW-C31-03).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Context:</em> My first launch as the lead analyst; the onboarding flow had six steps; a senior designer wanted to keep all six because each had a purpose; I believed two steps were hurting completion. Stakes: the flow decided whether a new user ever saw the product; completion was 41% (invented).</li>\n<li><em>Decision:</em> Argue my view harder, escalate to the product manager, or propose a test that could settle it. I chose the test, because the designer's point (that each step helps later use) was plausible and I had only correlational data.</li>\n<li><em>Action:</em> I asked the designer what each step was for and wrote down her reasons; found that two steps collected data used by a feature that only 9% of users reached; proposed an experiment with a version that moved those two steps to after first use; agreed on metrics together (completion as the goal, seven-day activation as the guardrail); she suggested a change to the copy that I added.</li>\n<li><em>Result:</em> Completion rose from 41% to 47% and seven-day activation was flat (the interval for the change in activation included zero); we shipped the shorter flow and moved the two steps. The designer's copy change contributed a point of the lift.</li>\n<li><em>Learning:</em> I had started by assuming the steps were the problem; I now start by asking what a step is for. Also, I now agree the success and guardrail metrics with the person I disagree with before running the test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>As told (about 2 minutes 30 seconds).</strong> &quot;In my first launch as lead analyst, I disagreed with a senior designer about our onboarding flow. It had six steps and only 41% of new users finished. I thought two of the steps were costing us completions; she thought each step mattered. My choice was to argue harder, go to the product manager, or find a way to test it. I chose to test, because her point was plausible and I only had correlations. First I asked her what each step was for and wrote it down.</p>\n<p>That showed that two steps collected data for a feature only 9% of users reached. I proposed an experiment moving those two steps to after first use, and we agreed together on the metrics: completion as the goal, and seven-day activation as a guardrail. She suggested a change to the copy, which I added. Completion rose from 41% to 47%, activation was flat, and we shipped it. About a point of that lift came from her copy change. What I took from it is to ask what a step is for before deciding it is the problem, and to agree the guardrail with the person I disagree with before the test.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Signals.</strong> Respect for the other view (asked, wrote it down, used her idea); a real decision (test, not escalate); evidence with limits (&quot;the interval included zero&quot;); credit given; learning that is behavioral. A weaker version: &quot;We disagreed, I showed her the data, and she agreed.&quot; It would give no decision, no action and no learning.</p>\n<p><strong>Probes to prepare.</strong> What was the sample size and duration? What if activation had dropped? How did you feel when she pushed back? Did the result change her mind?</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The decision part (why test) is what makes it a PM story, and crediting the colleague makes the conflict credible.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-3-a-failure-story-and-the-probe-ladder-illust\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — A failure story and the probe ladder (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt (invented story).</strong> &quot;Tell me about a decision you got wrong.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>The failure-story test (FW-C31-06).</strong> <em>Real cost:</em> a roadmap decision was delayed two weeks (invented) and a stakeholder lost some trust in my numbers. <em>My decision:</em> I reported a result without reconciling it. <em>Honest reason it seemed right:</em> the lift was large and it matched what the team expected. <em>Repair:</em> I told the stakeholder the same day I found the error, reran the analysis, sent a correction. <em>Behavior change:</em> I now reconcile any headline number against an independent source before sharing, and I have a short checklist.</p>\n<p><strong>As told (about 2 minutes 30 seconds).</strong> &quot;Early in my current job I told the team that a new notification design had raised retention by 6 points. It was a big number and it matched what everyone hoped, and I shared it in a team meeting without checking it against anything else. Two days later I was preparing the readout for the director and noticed that the treatment group's events were counted twice because of a logging change that shipped with the test. After correcting it, the lift was 1.1 points. I told my manager and the product manager that afternoon, sent a written correction, and reran the analysis with the engineer who owned the logging. The cost was real: a roadmap decision that had leaned on the 6 points was delayed by two weeks, and a stakeholder told me she would now want a second pair of eyes on my numbers. What I do differently is reconcile any headline figure against a second source before I share it, and I check event counts per user between groups. I used it last quarter, when a dashboard figure didn't match finance's; I found a definition difference before it went into a slide.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Probe ladder (FW-C31-05).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Level 1, detail.</em> &quot;What exactly was wrong in the logging?&quot; — &quot;A new event fired once on the server and once on the client in the treatment arm; I found it by comparing events per user between arms, which were 1.9 times higher in treatment.&quot; &quot;How did you tell people?&quot; — &quot;In person to my manager first, then a written note to the thread with the corrected number and the cause.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Level 2, reasoning.</em> &quot;Why did you share before checking?&quot; — &quot;The number matched our hopes and I was keen to give good news; I treated plausibility as verification. I also didn't want to hold up a meeting.&quot; &quot;How did you decide the corrected number was right?&quot; — &quot;Two independent counts agreed after the fix, and the engineer confirmed the event schema.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>Level 3, counterfactual and limits.</em> &quot;Would the decision have been different if the true figure had been known?&quot; — &quot;Probably the same direction but slower: 1.1 points was still positive, but smaller than the cost of one of the options, so they would have chosen differently.&quot; &quot;Is there anything you still don't know?&quot; — &quot;Whether the logging error affected earlier tests; I checked the last three and found none.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A failure with a real cost, an honest reason, a repair and a habit with evidence that it is used.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-4-building-a-story-bank-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Building a story bank (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The inventory (FW-C31-04).</strong> A candidate with about two years of experience lists 12 episodes (invented): (1) the first funnel dashboard and form cut; (2) the notification default experiment; (3) the onboarding disagreement with a designer; (4) the double-counted events error; (5) a user-interview project that changed a feature's scope; (6) a launch that missed its date after an estimate was cut by management; (7) mentoring a new analyst; (8) a campus hackathon app with a team of four; (9) pushing back on a request to use a pre-checked subscription box; (10) a pricing-page test that failed; (11) helping support build a report that cut ticket time; (12) a quarter in which three requests competed for one engineer.</p>\n<p><strong>Selection.</strong> Keep episodes with a real decision, clear stakes, an outcome, a learning and a story you can tell truthfully for ten minutes. Drop (7) (little decision), (11) (mostly someone else's work) and (8) (too old and too far from the role), keeping 9.</p>\n<p><strong>Mapping to themes.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Theme</th>\n<th>Primary story</th>\n<th>Backup</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Conflict</td>\n<td>(3) the onboarding disagreement</td>\n<td>(6) the missed date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td>(4) the double-counted events</td>\n<td>(10) the failed pricing test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Influence without authority</td>\n<td>(2) the notification default</td>\n<td>(1) the form cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ambiguity</td>\n<td>(5) the user-interview project</td>\n<td>(12) the three requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer focus</td>\n<td>(5) the user-interview project</td>\n<td>(1) the form cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data-driven decision</td>\n<td>(2) the notification default</td>\n<td>(10) the failed pricing test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prioritization</td>\n<td>(12) the three competing requests</td>\n<td>(6) the missed date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leading others</td>\n<td>(6) the launch, where I coordinated four people</td>\n<td>(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Values and judgment</td>\n<td>(9) the pre-checked box</td>\n<td>none yet</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Coverage check.</strong> Nine themes and nine stories, with some stories carrying two themes. Gaps: no backup for values (develop (9) with a second angle, and look for a second episode); (5) carries two themes, so choose which to lead with in a given loop. No story appears more than twice as a primary.</p>\n<p><strong>Use the attribute map (FW-C31-01).</strong> For a loop at a company that publishes a leadership list including ownership, disagreement and commitment, and results (Amazon-type), lead with (3), (4) and (2); for a company stressing growth mindset (Microsoft-type), lead with (4), (10) and (5). The stories do not change; the order and emphasis do.</p>\n<p><strong>Write notes, not scripts.</strong> Each of the nine stories gets five lines (context, decision, action, result, learning), the key numbers, two probes with answers, and what to omit.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Nine well-prepared stories cover the themes, and the bank is organized by decision and evidence, not by job title.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-worked-example-5-a-values-scenario-pressure-to-hit-a-number-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — A values scenario: pressure to hit a number (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Your manager wants to hit this quarter's sign-up target by pre-checking a box that subscribes people to a weekly email. You think it is manipulative. What do you do?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Apply the frame (FW-C31-08).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Facts.</em> What does the data say? A small test (invented): with the box pre-checked, sign-ups per 1,000 visitors rise from 80 to 101 (from 8.0% to 10.1%), but 7-day unsubscribes rise from 12% to 31%, and complaints triple. I also check the law and company policy: consent rules in several regions require opt-in for marketing email <code>[V: GDPR Article 4(11) (clear affirmative action) and the ePrivacy Directive (opt-in for marketing email), as summarized in guidance]</code>.</li>\n<li><em>Stakeholders and harms.</em> Users who did not intend to subscribe (annoyance, loss of trust); the company (complaints, spam reports, regulatory risk); the team's credibility.</li>\n<li><em>The pressure and the legitimate goal.</em> The target is a growth number; the legitimate goal is more engaged subscribers.</li>\n<li><em>The real effect.</em> Retained subscribers per 1,000 visitors after a week: before, 80 × (1 − 0.12) = 70.4; after, 101 × (1 − 0.31) = 69.7. The tactic produces no net gain and adds complaints and risk. It raises the number that is reported and not the number that matters (Goodhart, C05).</li>\n<li><em>Options.</em> Comply; refuse; propose an opt-in design that improves the value proposition (show a sample email, explain the benefit), test it against the pre-checked version on retained subscribers and complaints, and report the retained-subscribers measure to leadership.</li>\n<li><em>Principle.</em> A default that works against users' stated interests is a dark pattern (C10.10).</li>\n<li><em>Action and escalation.</em> Talk to my manager first, privately, with the numbers; propose the test and a revised target definition (retained subscribers); if told to ship it anyway and it appears to violate policy or law, raise it with legal or the policy owner, in writing, and decline to implement what I believe is unlawful.</li>\n<li><em>Cost I accept.</em> A harder conversation, a possible missed sign-up target this quarter, and the risk of being seen as difficult.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>As told (about 2 minutes).</strong> &quot;I would first check the facts, then talk to my manager with the numbers. In a small test, pre-checking the box raised sign-ups per thousand visitors from 80 to 101, but unsubscribes within a week went from 12% to 31%, so after a week we kept about 70 subscribers per thousand either way, with three times the complaints. So the tactic moves the number we report and not the number we want. I would propose an opt-in version that shows a sample of the email, test it against the pre-checked one on retained subscribers and complaints, and suggest we measure the target on retained subscribers. If I were told to ship the pre-checked box anyway and I believed it broke consent rules, I would raise it with legal in writing and not build it. It could cost us the sign-up target this quarter and some goodwill with my manager, and I would accept that.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The answer does not preach: it finds the legitimate goal, measures the real effect, offers a better route and states the cost.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Tell me about a time you disagreed with your manager.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;Once my manager wanted to launch a feature and I thought we should wait. We talked about it, and in the end we found a compromise that worked for everyone. It taught me the importance of communication.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no context or stakes, no decision, no action, no result, no number; a generic learning. It gives the interviewer nothing to probe.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;Last year my manager wanted to launch a redesigned checkout in the same week as a pricing change. I was concerned that we could not tell which change caused any movement in conversion, and that a problem would be hard to diagnose. I raised it in our one-to-one with a specific example from an earlier launch where two changes overlapped and we spent two weeks untangling the result. She worried about missing a seasonal window. We agreed to stagger the launches by a week, with the checkout first, which cost her four days against the window. Conversion rose 2.1% on the checkout alone, and the pricing change a week later was read cleanly. What I took from it is to bring a concrete example and a proposal, not only a concern.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I want to be honest that I was partly wrong. When I raised it, I assumed the seasonal window was flexible. My manager showed me it was worth about 8% of the quarter's volume, and that changed my proposal: instead of delaying the pricing change by a week, we launched it to a tenth of users the same week so that we captured most of the window while keeping a clean read from the other nine tenths. That was her idea built on my concern. When we disagreed over the final design, she decided, and I committed: I helped write the launch plan even though I preferred a longer delay.</p>\n<p>&quot;The result was a clean read of both changes and 90% of the window's volume. What I changed afterward is that I now ask what a deadline is worth in numbers before I argue against it, and I agree in advance what evidence would make us reverse. When she asked what I would do differently, I said I would have brought the cost of the delay in numbers to the first conversation, not discovered it in the second.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c31-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Names stakes, a decision, an action and a number</td>\n<td>Admits where you were partly wrong and uses the other person's idea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gives a concrete example and a proposal</td>\n<td>Shows commitment after the decision went the other way</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offers a learning</td>\n<td>Gives a behavioral change and its use since</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c31-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats and published values change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes sixteen Leadership Principles on its careers site and describes using them every day <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles, as cited by several summaries]</code>. Candidates commonly report that behavioral questions probe these principles, often with several rounds and detailed follow-ups <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: prepare eight to ten stories with numbers and your personal decisions (ownership, dive deep, disagreeing and committing, delivering results), and expect repeated probing for detail; do not recite principle names.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google describes assessing general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership and an attribute called Googleyness (not to be confused with culture fit, by Google's own clarification) in its hiring materials <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>. Candidates commonly report structured behavioral questions about ambiguity, collaboration and leadership without authority <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: choose stories with unclear goals and several stakeholders, show how you structured the problem and brought others along, and stay humble about what you did not know.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's published values emphasize moving fast, long-term impact and direct communication <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta's careers culture page, six core values]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions about impact, speed and tradeoffs, and about giving and receiving direct feedback <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: lead with results and numbers, show a speed-quality tradeoff you made and what it cost, and give an example of direct, respectful candor.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's culture materials emphasize a growth mindset (learning and curiosity over knowing everything) and collaboration <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions about learning from mistakes, working across teams and customer focus <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: include stories in which you changed your mind or learned something that changed your approach, and show how you helped others learn.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple does not publish a hiring rubric; candidates commonly report an emphasis on craft, attention to detail, collaboration across disciplines and discretion <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: have a story about obsessing over a detail and its cost, one about protecting users or privacy against a business request, and be careful not to disclose confidential information from past employers in any story.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix publishes a culture memo (last updated June 2024) built on four principles, with candor among its values and managers expected to give people context <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on independent judgment, candid feedback and decisions made without waiting for approval <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: prepare a story where you gave candid feedback to someone more senior, and one where you made a call on your own judgment and owned the outcome.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe publishes operating principles that include users first and moving with urgency and focus <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe careers pages, principles list; also quoted by The Pragmatic Engineer and Aced]</code>, and is widely described as valuing clear thinking and writing <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager]</code>. Candidates report behavioral questions on rigor and care for users, especially developers <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: show stories with precise reasoning and care about the user's real problem, and be ready to explain a decision clearly in writing-style structure.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c31-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A rambling introduction</td>\n<td>Four minutes, no arc</td>\n<td>Present, past in steps with reasons, future, hand over</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A generic motive for PM</td>\n<td>&quot;I like solving problems&quot;</td>\n<td>Evidence of what drew you and what you learned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>All &quot;we&quot;</td>\n<td>The interviewer cannot tell what you did</td>\n<td>&quot;I&quot; for your decisions and actions; credit others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>No decision</td>\n<td>A story that is a sequence of events</td>\n<td>State the options and why you chose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>No numbers</td>\n<td>&quot;It went well&quot;</td>\n<td>A figure with a baseline, or an honest &quot;I don't have it&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A fake failure or weakness</td>\n<td>&quot;I work too hard&quot;</td>\n<td>A real cost, an owned decision, a changed habit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Reciting company principles</td>\n<td>&quot;That shows ownership&quot;</td>\n<td>Tell the story; let them label it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Inventing detail</td>\n<td>A number that changes under probing</td>\n<td>Use only what is true; say what you don't recall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Defensiveness under probing</td>\n<td>Arguing with the follow-up</td>\n<td>Acknowledge the flaw; say what you now think</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Answering what you want to tell</td>\n<td>A story for a different theme</td>\n<td>Answer the question asked; say so if you pivot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c31-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> and <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> are from C02, and the pre-mortem (<a href=\"#gl-pre-mortem\">pre-mortem</a>) is from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Steelmanning</strong> (<code>MM-steelman</code>). State the other side's position in its strongest form before responding. In a conflict story it shows respect and understanding: &quot;Her point was that each step helps later use, which was plausible,&quot; and it makes your resolution credible.</p>\n<p><strong>Hanlon's razor</strong> (<code>MM-hanlon</code>). Do not attribute to malice what is adequately explained by misunderstanding or competing constraints <code>[V: Robert J. Hanlon, in Arthur Bloch, Murphy's Law Book Two, 1980, page 52; earlier similar lines: Heinlein, Logic of Empire, 1941; Goethe, 1774. The book wording is an adaptation of the original, which says stupidity]</code>. Tell conflict stories as differences in information and incentives, not villains.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). To prepare, ask how an answer would fail: no decision, no number, all &quot;we,&quot; a probe you cannot answer. Then fix each in the story.</p>\n<p><strong>The pre-mortem</strong> (<code>MM-pre-mortem</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-pre-mortem\">pre-mortem</a>, C02). For a values scenario, imagine the decision was regretted a year later and ask why; the answers (a missing fact, a harm you ignored) are what to check now.</p>\n<span id=\"c31-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<p>Your stories involve other people, and they will describe the same events differently. Test each story against how they would tell it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> An engineer in your story would care about what you asked of them and whether you listened to the constraint. If you say you &quot;convinced engineering,&quot; ask whether they would say they were persuaded or pushed. Credit their contribution in the story.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> A designer would care about whether you took their reasoning seriously. Tell conflict stories so that the designer would recognize their own view in your account of it.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> A data scientist would check the numbers and the claim about causation. Be precise about what the data showed and what it did not.</li>\n<li><strong>Your manager.</strong> A manager would check scope and credit: did you do this, or did you only report it? Do not claim a decision that was your manager's; describe your part.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c31-checklist-before-you-walk-into-a-behavioral-round\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you walk into a behavioral round</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Do I have a two-minute introduction with a clear arc and three proof points I can expand on?</li>\n<li>Do I have honest, specific reasons for product management and for this company and role?</li>\n<li>Do I have eight to ten stories, each with notes on context, decision, action, result and learning, and verified numbers?</li>\n<li>Does my bank cover conflict, failure, influence, ambiguity, customer focus, a data-driven decision, prioritization, leading and a values moment?</li>\n<li>For each story, can I say what I did, as opposed to what the team did?</li>\n<li>Have I prepared two probes at each of three levels for every story, and one thing that went wrong?</li>\n<li>Do I have a real failure, a real weakness and a values story that cost something?</li>\n<li>Have I rehearsed aloud, timed to two to three minutes, and mapped stories to the company's published attributes without reciting them?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c31-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c31-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C31-01 · The answer that said &quot;we&quot; forty times · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A candidate with two years of experience practices the prompt &quot;Tell me about a project you led&quot; with a friend who has interviewed candidates. The candidate talks for five minutes about a redesign of a reporting dashboard.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A project the candidate cared about; a habit of describing team outcomes; no practice at timing.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The friend counts: &quot;we&quot; appears about 40 times and &quot;I&quot; six times; there is no number in the story; the friend cannot say what the candidate decided; the story's learning is &quot;teamwork is important.&quot; The friend's note: &quot;I don't know if you led it or watched it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The story contains no decision, no personal action and no result, so the listener can infer nothing about the candidate's judgment.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The content was probably good. The telling hid it. Using the anatomy (FW-C31-03): <em>Context</em> took three minutes; <em>Decision</em> was missing; <em>Action</em> was narrated as &quot;we&quot;; <em>Result</em> had no number; <em>Learning</em> was generic. The probe test: ask &quot;what did you personally decide?&quot; and &quot;what was the result?&quot;; the candidate answers both well when asked, which shows the material exists. The rewrite: two sentences of context (the dashboard took four minutes to load and 60% of managers stopped using it); the decision (rebuild everything or cut it to five key charts, and I chose to cut, after interviewing six managers); the action (the interviews, the proposal, getting the data team to build a cached view); the result (load time to 8 seconds, weekly use from 40% to 71% of managers); the learning (talk to users before choosing scope).</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep the story as it is</td>\n<td>Comfort</td>\n<td>No signal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Rewrite with the five parts and numbers, and rehearse to three minutes</td>\n<td>Signal</td>\n<td>Work to recall the numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Switch to a different story</td>\n<td>Possibly a better story</td>\n<td>Loses the stronger material</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs an evening and turns a good project into a good story; C is unnecessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Write the five-part notes, replace each &quot;we&quot; with the specific &quot;I&quot; or a named role where true, add the baseline and result numbers, and time it at 2 minutes 40 seconds. Prepare two probes at each level.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the rewritten story ran 2 minutes 40 seconds with 14 uses of &quot;I&quot; for personal actions and 9 of &quot;we&quot; for team results, included the baseline and the result numbers, and survived five minutes of probing by the friend (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the candidate had in fact made no decisions on the project, the honest choice would have been a different story. A made-up decision would have collapsed under the probe &quot;why not rebuild everything?&quot; The lesson: a story is evidence of what you did; tell the decision, the action and the number, and use only what is true.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c31-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C31-02 · Johnson &amp; Johnson, 1982: a values decision with a known cost · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): a contemporaneous Fortune article, &quot;The fight to save Tylenol&quot; by Thomas Moore (29 November 1982), read in full; the Food and Drug Administration's compliance policy guide on tamper-resistant packaging (CPG 450.500) and the Federal Register notices of the 1982 rule; a university ethics case study of the recall (University of New Mexico, Daniels Fund Ethics Initiative), for later share figures and the company's Credo; and educational and journalistic summaries for the 1983 anti-tampering law. Sources differ on some figures (noted below), and none gives the internal deliberations in detail. The analysis is the author's. The case is used to show the shape of a values decision, not to praise or judge a company. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> In late September 1982 seven people in the Chicago area died after taking Extra-Strength Tylenol capsules that had been laced with cyanide, in what investigators concluded was tampering after the bottles reached store shelves, since the poisoned bottles came from different factories. Tylenol, made by a Johnson &amp; Johnson subsidiary, was a leading pain reliever, with a market share reported as about 35 to 37 percent, and by one account accounted for 15 to 20 percent of the company's profits. Johnson &amp; Johnson's chairman, James Burke, took charge of the response personally, met the FBI and the FDA in Washington on the Monday after the first weekend, and, on the Monday after the second weekend, formed a strategic group of key executives to oversee the work of rebuilding the brand <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>. The company used the media to warn the public and urged people to switch to tablets <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>. Burke began to advocate recalling all Extra-Strength capsules, but the FBI and the FDA counseled against a precipitous recall: the FBI feared it would tell the perpetrator he was winning, and the FDA feared it would cause more public anxiety than it would relieve <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>.</p>\n<p>On the following Tuesday (5 October, according to reference summaries), after what appeared to be a copycat strychnine poisoning in California, the FDA agreed with Burke that all Tylenol capsules should be recalled nationwide: about 31 million bottles with a retail value of over $100 million, a cost the company estimated at about $100 million before taxes, which cut third-quarter earnings per share from 78 cents the year before to 51 cents (Fortune). By late November 1982 the company had a new tamper-resistant package and was preparing to bring capsules back, and sales of Tylenol products had initially fallen about 80% <code>[V: Fortune, 29 November 1982]</code>; accounts differ on exactly when the product returned to the shelves. Tylenol's share is reported to have fallen from 37% of the over-the-counter pain reliever market to about 7% by late 1982 and to have recovered to about 30% within six months (the University of New Mexico case study), while other accounts give 8% and about a year <code>[V: UNM case study; secondary accounts]</code>. The FDA published final tamper-resistant packaging regulations for most over-the-counter drugs on 5 November 1982, about five weeks after the first deaths, and amended them in 1989 to require two tamper-resistant features for two-piece capsules unless they are sealed by a tamper-resistant technology <code>[V: FDA CPG 450.500; Federal Register]</code>. Congress passed an anti-tampering law in 1983, making product tampering a federal crime <code>[V: reference summaries]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A product central to the company's profits and identity; uncertainty about whether the contamination was local or national, and who was responsible; public fear; a media environment moving by the hour; a company credo that put customers first, which the committee used as a guide, according to later case studies (Fortune's contemporaneous account does not mention it); and the fact that the poisoning occurred after the product left the company's control, which would have allowed a narrower response.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the deaths, the share and profit figures, the recall's scale and cost, the earnings effect, the share recovery, the packaging change and the rule change. Accounts differ on the exact share figures (Fortune: a 35% share of a $1 billion analgesic market in 1982, and an estimated 7% of J&amp;J's worldwide sales and 15% to 20% of its profits in 1981; the New Mexico case study: 37%). Fortune ties the nationwide capsule recall to the California incident, and reports that company surveys found 94% of consumers aware of the poisonings, 87% of Tylenol users saying the maker was not responsible, and 61% saying they were unlikely to buy Extra-Strength capsules in future <code>[V: Fortune, 1982]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The company faced a choice between a narrow response (withdrawal in Chicago, warnings) that would limit cost and a broad one (a national recall) that would cost far more and remove any chance that another person would be harmed. As an observable problem: &quot;Seven people had died, the cause was unknown to the public, and further harm was possible.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, using the values frame (FW-C31-08).</em></p>\n<p><em>Facts first.</em> The company did not wait for certainty about the source of the problem; it acted on the risk to people while investigation went on, and it cooperated with authorities and the press.</p>\n";
